"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,928\nThe Learning Brain\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,570\nEpisode 16\n\n3\n00:00:07,650 --> 00:00:09,463\nSocial learning\n\n4\n00:00:12,263 --> 00:00:13,738\nIn nineteen sixty one,\n\n5\n00:00:13,812 --> 00:00:17,901\npreschool children watched an adult attack an inflatable Bobo doll: punching it,\n\n6\n00:00:18,219 --> 00:00:21,136\nsitting on it, striking it with a mallet.\n\n7\n00:00:21,626 --> 00:00:23,410\nLater, alone with the doll,\n\n8\n00:00:23,515 --> 00:00:25,697\nmany of them copied those exact,\n\n9\n00:00:25,717 --> 00:00:27,124\nunusual actions.\n\n10\n00:00:27,450 --> 00:00:29,121\nNobody had rewarded them.\n\n11\n00:00:29,521 --> 00:00:31,336\nThey learned just by watching.\n\n12\n00:00:31,506 --> 00:00:34,555\nBandura argued that watching is not passive.\n\n13\n00:00:35,079 --> 00:00:37,249\nThe learner has to attend to the model,\n\n14\n00:00:37,424 --> 00:00:38,619\nretain what they saw,\n\n15\n00:00:38,639 --> 00:00:40,188\nbe able to reproduce it,\n\n16\n00:00:40,306 --> 00:00:41,493\nand have a reason to.\n\n17\n00:00:41,981 --> 00:00:43,346\nIf any one of these fails,\n\n18\n00:00:43,366 --> 00:00:45,091\nthe learning does not show up.\n\n19\n00:00:45,527 --> 00:00:47,469\nSeeing is only the first step.\n\n20\n00:00:47,639 --> 00:00:48,998\nIn the nineteen nineties,\n\n21\n00:00:49,212 --> 00:00:57,942\nresearchers recording single cells in the premotor cortex of macaque monkeys found neurons that fired both when the monkey grasped an object,\n\n22\n00:00:58,411 --> 00:01:00,694\nand when it watched someone else grasp one.\n\n23\n00:01:01,240 --> 00:01:03,224\nThey called them mirror neurons.\n\n24\n00:01:03,610 --> 00:01:05,742\nIn the most detailed early study,\n\n25\n00:01:05,975 --> 00:01:09,093\nthey were about seventeen percent of the cells sampled.\n\n26\n00:01:09,263 --> 00:01:10,350\nHere is the caution.\n\n27\n00:01:10,784 --> 00:01:13,517\nClaims that mirror neurons explain empathy,\n\n28\n00:01:13,532 --> 00:01:16,760\nlanguage, or human learning went far beyond the data.\n\n29\n00:01:17,254 --> 00:01:20,074\nMost human evidence comes from brain imaging,\n\n30\n00:01:20,326 --> 00:01:25,813\nnot single cells, and whether these cells are needed to understand actions is contested.\n\n31\n00:01:26,394 --> 00:01:30,679\nThe behavioral finding stands on its own: people learn by watching.\n\n32\n00:01:31,103 --> 00:01:33,968\nWe do not need the mirror neuron story to use it.\n\n33\n00:01:34,138 --> 00:01:36,904\nIn a classroom, that finding has a direct form.\n\n34\n00:01:37,242 --> 00:01:41,871\nFor novices, studying worked examples beats solving the same problems unaided.\n\n35\n00:01:42,260 --> 00:01:44,051\nThis is the worked example effect.\n\n36\n00:01:44,530 --> 00:01:46,800\nAs skill grows, the advantage fades,\n\n37\n00:01:46,820 --> 00:01:48,464\nand practice should take over.\n\n38\n00:01:48,824 --> 00:01:50,989\nWatching expert help can work too.\n\n39\n00:01:51,394 --> 00:01:54,106\nStudents who watched videos of tutoring in pairs,\n\n40\n00:01:54,126 --> 00:01:57,940\ntalking it through, learned as much as the students being tutored.\n\n41\n00:01:58,460 --> 00:02:00,954\nThose who watched alone learned less.\n\n42\n00:02:01,124 --> 00:02:02,499\nPeers matter as well.\n\n43\n00:02:02,873 --> 00:02:03,877\nIn a genetics course,\n\n44\n00:02:03,897 --> 00:02:05,636\nstudents answered a clicker question,\n\n45\n00:02:05,656 --> 00:02:07,141\ndiscussed it with neighbors,\n\n46\n00:02:07,374 --> 00:02:10,097\nthen answered a new question on the same concept,\n\n47\n00:02:10,117 --> 00:02:10,600\nalone.\n\n48\n00:02:11,032 --> 00:02:15,494\nScores improved, even in groups where no one had known the answer at first.\n\n49\n00:02:15,816 --> 00:02:18,916\nAnd just expecting to teach changes how people study.\n\n50\n00:02:19,377 --> 00:02:22,481\nLearners told they would teach a passage recalled more of it,\n\n51\n00:02:22,693 --> 00:02:24,125\nand organized it better,\n\n52\n00:02:24,145 --> 00:02:26,165\nthan learners expecting a test.\n\n53\n00:02:26,577 --> 00:02:28,151\nNobody actually taught.\n\n54\n00:02:28,321 --> 00:02:29,108\nFor teaching.\n\n55\n00:02:29,440 --> 00:02:31,010\nShow worked examples early,\n\n56\n00:02:31,030 --> 00:02:33,455\nthen fade them as students gain skill.\n\n57\n00:02:33,935 --> 00:02:37,075\nThink aloud: make your expert reasoning visible,\n\n58\n00:02:37,121 --> 00:02:38,847\nincluding the dead ends.\n\n59\n00:02:39,161 --> 00:02:42,144\nUse peer discussion before you reveal the answer.\n\n60\n00:02:42,492 --> 00:02:45,692\nAnd give students reasons to explain to someone else.\n\n61\n00:02:45,862 --> 00:02:47,635\nWe are built to learn from each other.\n\n62\n00:02:47,988 --> 00:02:50,020\nLet students see how experts think,\n\n63\n00:02:50,040 --> 00:02:51,432\nand let them explain.\n\n"
